      {
        "id": 91274,
        "name": "Arcanist Lupus",
        "username": "Arcanist_Lupus",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arcanist_lupus/{size}/2169_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-02-14T05:22:08.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>The problem is that associations can be stronger than meanings. &nbsp;Yes, the ad is using the literal meaning of \"loser\", and it's easy to recognize what they mean. &nbsp;But that doesn't prevent the association \"Playing SotM = Loser(at life)\". &nbsp;It won't be a strong association, but it will still be there, and it's not an association we want made at all.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>That being said, if ThinkGeek has a deprecating sense of humor, then that goes a great way to breaking the association. &nbsp;It's like calling your friend a jerk - you are familiar enough with each other that you know that term is meant in a meaningless fashion, and no negative connotation is attached to the insult.<br><br>It also helps that \"League of Losers\" feels like a reference, even if the readers aren't familiar with it, which also helps break the negative connotations.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>I do trust ThinkGeek though. &nbsp;They've had a lot of success selling to the geek community, which is a community with a particularly strong negative association with the term \"loser\". &nbsp;It's not something that would slip under the radar. &nbsp;If it's there, it's because they don't think it's a problem.</p>",
